The aPS repeat unit contains one \(\mathrm{CH_2}\) group and one \(\mathrm{CH(C_6H_5)}\) group. Using the amorphous group contributions at 298 K:

\[
V_a=16.37+84.16=100.53\ \mathrm{cm^3/mol}
\]

\[
V_w=10.23+52.6=62.83\ \mathrm{cm^3/mol}
\]

Using the standard occupied-volume estimate \(V_0=1.3V_w\),

\[
\mathrm{FFV}=\frac{V_a-V_0}{V_a}
=\frac{100.53-1.3(62.83)}{100.53}
\]

\[
\boxed{0.188}
\]

Assuming the component volumes are additive and there are no voids, the density is found from the mass-fraction rule of mixtures:

\[
\frac{1}{\rho_c}
=\frac{0.67}{1.14}+\frac{0.33}{2.54}
=0.71764.
\]

Therefore, the composite density in \(\mathrm{Mg/m^3}\) is

\[
\boxed{1.39}
\]

Assuming polyacetal means polyoxymethylene, its repeat unit is \(-\mathrm{CH_2-O}-\), with a mass of approximately \(30.03\) amu. Neglecting end groups,

\[
\text{degree\_of\_polymerization}
\approx \frac{25{,}000}{30.03}
\approx \boxed{833}
\]

Assuming a three-point bend, with thickness \(t=6\ \mathrm{mm}\) and width \(b=15\ \mathrm{mm}\), the initial slope is \(m=538\ \mathrm{N/mm}\). The flexural modulus is

\[
E_f=\frac{L^3m}{4bt^3}
=\frac{60^3(538)}{4(15)(6^3)}
=8966.7\ \mathrm{MPa}.
\]

**Caveat:** The stated specimen length of 50 mm is shorter than the 60 mm support span, so the test geometry is physically inconsistent. The result is the nominal value calculated from the supplied numbers.

\[
\text{flexural\_modulus}=\boxed{8.97\times10^3}
\]

At \(10^6\) cycles, the room-temperature, tensile-only curve gives an allowable stress of approximately \(49\ \text{MPa}\).

Rearranging \(\sigma=pr/(2t)\),

\[
p_{\max}=\frac{2t\sigma}{r}
=\frac{2(2)(49)}{30}
\approx 6.5\ \text{MPa}.
\]

\[
\boxed{6.5}
\]

Using mole fractions of the repeat units, the molar masses are \(28.05\) for polyethylene (\(\mathrm{C_2H_4}\)) and \(62.50\) for PVC (\(\mathrm{C_2H_3Cl}\)).

\[
\text{PVC weight percentage}
=100\frac{0.20(62.50)}{0.20(62.50)+0.80(28.05)}
\]

\[
\boxed{35.8}
\]

The mass of one repeat unit is

\[
16(12.01)+14(1.008)+3(16.00)=254.27\ \mathrm{amu}.
\]

Dividing the polymer molecular weight by the repeat-unit mass gives

\[
\text{degree\_of\_polymerization}
=\frac{100{,}000}{254.27}
\approx \boxed{393.3}
\]

Assuming the component volumes are additive, the PTFE volume fraction is

\[
V_{\mathrm{PTFE}}
=\frac{1.54-1.42}{2.15-1.42}
=0.16438.
\]

Converting this to a weight percentage gives

\[
\mathrm{weight\_percent\_PTFE}
=\frac{0.16438(2.15)}{1.54}\times 100
=\boxed{22.95}
\]

Assuming open-circuit conditions, the compressive stress is

\[
|\sigma_{33}|=\frac{F}{A}
=\frac{100}{\pi(1.0\times10^{-3})^2}
=3.18\times10^7\ \mathrm{Pa}.
\]

With \(D_3=0\), the piezoelectric relation gives

\[
|E_3|=\frac{d_{33}|\sigma_{33}|}{\varepsilon_0\varepsilon_{r33}^{X}}
=\frac{(289\times10^{-12})(3.18\times10^7)}
{(8.854\times10^{-12})(1300)}
\approx 7.99\times10^5\ \mathrm{V/m}.
\]

Multiplying by the thickness, \(5.0\times10^{-3}\,\mathrm{m}\), gives the voltage magnitude:

\[
\boxed{4.00\times10^3}
\]

An HDPE repeat unit is \(\mathrm{C_2H_4}\). Replacing \(8\%\) of its hydrogen atoms gives the average composition

\[
\mathrm{C_2H_{4(0.92)}Cl_{4(0.08)}}=\mathrm{C_2H_{3.68}Cl_{0.32}}.
\]

The chlorine concentration in wt% is therefore

\[
100\frac{0.32(35.45)}
{2(12.01)+3.68(1.008)+0.32(35.45)}
=\boxed{29.03}
\]

For an Arrhenius process, the jump rate is proportional to \(e^{-Q/(RT)}\). Thus,

\[
\frac{Q}{R}
=
\frac{\ln\!\left((8\times10^{10})/(5\times10^8)\right)}
{1/773-1/1073}
\approx 1.4032\times10^4\ \mathrm{K}.
\]

Using \(R=1.987\ \mathrm{cal/(mol\cdot K)}\), the activation energy in cal/mol is

\[
\boxed{2.79\times10^4}
\]

For a rectangular specimen in three-point bending, using the support span \(L=4\) in:

1. The flexural strength is
\[
\sigma_f=\frac{3FL}{2bh^2}
=\frac{3(400)(4)}{2(0.50)(0.25)^2}
=76{,}800\ \text{psi}.
\]

2. Assuming linear-elastic bending, the flexural modulus is
\[
E_f=\frac{FL^3}{4bh^3\delta}
=\frac{400(4)^3}{4(0.50)(0.25)^3(0.037)}
\approx 2.214\times10^7\ \text{psi}.
\]

\[
\boxed{(76800,\;2.214\times10^7)}
\]

For an internal flaw, the crack half-length is \(a=(1\,\text{mm})/2=5.0\times10^{-4}\,\text{m}\). Using \(f=1\),

\[
K_{\mathrm{Ic}}=f\sigma\sqrt{\pi a}
=25\sqrt{\pi(5.0\times10^{-4})}
=0.991\ \text{MPa}\sqrt{\text{m}}.
\]

\[
\text{plane\_strain\_fracture\_toughness}=\boxed{0.991}
\]
